+# Task 5 public storage, Jobs, network, and logging evidence
+
+Checkpoint: 2026-10-06T01:40:54Z. Public Microsoft Learn documentation only. No Azure, credential, or SEC calls. This is a documentation checkpoint, not a deployment/capacity verification. Sources were collected via web search before this checkpoint; precise retrieval timestamps were not emitted by the web tool, so metadata explicitly uses checkpoint time and does not assert an exact retrieval time. Short verbatim claim extracts and paraphrases are retained in `sources-v1.json`; SHA-256 metadata covers retained files rather than unretained origin HTML.
+
+## API and storage selections supported by retrieved documentation
+
+- Select a Standard general-purpose v2 account (`kind=StorageV2`, `sku=Standard_ZRS`), HNS enabled, Hot default tier. The account-create table explicitly permits HNS with Standard_ZRS; the account-overview workload table explicitly shows Standard GPv2 + HNS + Hot + ZRS for analytics. This is a supported configuration; no account was provisioned or interrogated.
+- Standard GPv2 supports Tables as an account service. The retrieved overview describes the account as containing blobs, files, queues, and tables and gives the Table endpoint. Combined with its HNS analytics configuration, this supports coexistence as a documentation inference. An explicit sentence that HNS does not disable Tables was not retrieved. Do not claim runtime coexistence was tested.
+- For each application-owned raw/manifest/lock object, use only Blob APIs throughout that object's lifetime: create/upload/overwrite with Put Blob or Put Block + Put Block List, Get Blob, properties/metadata, and conditional Delete Blob. Do not introduce DFS Path Create/append/flush, NFS, or SFTP writes to those same objects. HNS alone does not prohibit block blobs. The known-issues page says DFS-created objects cannot be overwritten with PutBlock/PutBlockList, although PutBlob can overwrite within its API-version size limit. A later block upload is therefore unsafe if any tool first creates that object through DFS. Delete Blob cannot recursively remove directories; list results can include directories and delimiter support is `/` only.
+- Put Block List updates overwrite properties/metadata, maintain an existing lease, and permit conditional requests. Its active lease ID must be supplied. Uncommitted blocks are collected after a week without successful Put Block/Put Block List calls; a Put Blob also collects them. Operational code should explicitly handle failed uploads and should not assume staged blocks are permanent.
+- Use finite Blob leases for mutual exclusion, renewing before expiry. Documentation supports 15–60 seconds or infinite leases. An active lease enforces exclusive writes/deletes to that blob and requires the lease ID on PutBlob, PutBlock, PutBlockList, and DeleteBlob; missing IDs fail with 412. A separate lock blob does not automatically protect any Table entity or other blob. Lease-lost workers must stop protected work; application fencing and conditional updates remain required.
+- For Table state compare-and-swap, send the exact retrieved ETag in If-Match on Update Entity. Changed ETags yield 412; reread/reconcile rather than unconditional retry. `If-Match: *` disables concurrency protection; omitting If-Match can invoke upsert for API versions 2011-08-18 and later. Do not substitute unconditional upsert for CAS.
+
+## Regional support limits
+
+Official Table reliability documentation requires Standard GPv2 for ZRS and allows zone-redundant accounts in regions supporting availability zones. Official region inventory lists `centralus`, `eastus`, and `eastus2` with zone support. Storage redundancy documentation recommends ZRS for Data Lake workloads. These together support the requested candidate configuration in the three named regions at documentation level. No authenticated regional SKU/capacity check was made. The region page reports East US 2 with four zones and a coming-soon indicator; do not infer availability of every zone or subscription capacity from that display. The retrieved GPv1 retirement unsupported-region list does not include these candidates, but it is only corroborating evidence, not a GPv2 per-region capability matrix.
+
+## Jobs resources and ephemeral storage
+
+Jobs documentation explicitly places resources.cpu/resources.memory in the job template and says apps/jobs share their environment networking and logging. Containers documentation covers Jobs behavior and the CPU/memory allocation table includes 2.0 vCPU + 4.0Gi. Current Consumption profiles can reach 4 vCPU/8GiB in workload-profile environments; 2 vCPU/4GiB is the maximum only for legacy Consumption-only environments. The selected 2 vCPU/4GiB allocation remains valid.
+
+**Unresolved documentation blocker:** retrieved storage-mounts documentation gives 8GiB ephemeral storage for replicas allocated over 1 vCPU, but phrases scope as a container app. I did not retrieve an explicit statement that this quota applies to manual Container Apps Jobs executions. Therefore 8GiB for the selected 2-vCPU manual Job is a plausible inference, not a finalized documented guarantee. Lead owns separate Jobs schema proof; avoid duplicating that evidence. Actual disk use/capacity remains Stage 7 work. The same storage page says Blob Storage file shares cannot be mounted; SDK streaming/downloads must not be described as a native Blob mount.
+
+## Network and logs
+
+Jobs share environment networking/logging. Default managed networking accesses internet endpoints. Workload-profile environments support UDR/NAT Gateway egress and private endpoints, while legacy Consumption-only environments do not support custom egress/NAT. Outbound public IPs may change; do not promise stable egress IP without appropriate custom networking. Storage HNS private-endpoint guidance calls for both blob and dfs endpoints, because some operations redirect or require dfs, even if application writes use Blob only. Configuration and access were not tested.
+
+The retrieved Log Analytics integration article names `ContainerAppConsoleLogs_CL` for stdout/stderr and `ContainerAppSystemLogs_CL` for platform system logs. These are the documented direct Log Analytics integration tables. **Unresolved:** no job-specific field/table routing proof was retrieved, and no Azure Monitor diagnostic-settings route/category/resource-specific-table proof was retrieved. Do not apply app-only `ContainerAppName_s` queries to Jobs without verifying the job identifier schema. Selected route must be tied to its documented tables; resource-specific table names must not be silently substituted.
+
+**Unresolved:** 90-day retention behavior and table exceptions were not retrieved. No evidence here proves that a workspace retention setting controls every selected table, nor that job execution history lasts 90 days. No alert-type source was retained; metric/log/activity alert selection remains unverified in this checkpoint.
+
+## Retrieval failures and limitations
+
+1. `curl -fsSL --max-time 30 https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/data-lake-storage-known-issues -o /private/tmp/task5-known-issues.html` failed in sandbox with exit 6: could not resolve learn.microsoft.com. No origin body retained.
+2. The same public download was submitted with require_escalated. Its encompassing functions call was aborted after 1715.6 seconds. Download outcome is unknown; the later web search batched after it (Jobs log tables, retention, alerts, HNS Tables) did not return results and must not be counted as successful source retrieval.
+3. The initial non-English known-issues result was official Microsoft Learn. Canonical en-us URL is supplied for reader access, but that canonical download did not succeed. Exact original source URL is retained in JSON.
+4. Hashes identify the retained claim extract artifact; they do not prove exact origin-document byte identity, publication version, or deployment behavior.

